WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.569
-My first guest is a global icon

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:05.171
who has won
every award imaginable.

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:08.241
She's the only artist
ever to have a number one album

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.376
in six consecutive decades.

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:11.343
[ Audience murmurs ]

00:00:11.343 --> 00:00:13.679
Her latest album,
"Release Me 2,"

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:15.047
is available now.

00:00:15.047 --> 00:00:18.918
Ladies and gentlemen, please
welcome Barbra Streisand!

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:19.986
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.754
Yeah!
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:25.324
Come on! Mwah! Mwah!
We love you!

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:27.827
Oh, thank you!
-Hi, everybody.

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:30.130
-I know. I'm sorry.
I'm too excited.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.532
Thank you so much for
coming back to the show.

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:35.034
-Oh, it's my pleasure.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:36.703
'Cause you're always fun
to talk to.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.105
-[ Laughs ]
Thank you. You too.

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.507
We have so much to talk about.

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:43.343
First of all, I want to say
congratulations

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.945
on your anniversary --
25 years together

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.513
with your husband James Brolin.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:48.514
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:50.216
Not too shabby.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.018
-Yeah, not to shabby is right.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:55.354
-Yeah, did you know right off
the bat that he was the one?

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:59.392
-Right off the bat --
Well, he told me he knew.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.361
You know, we were set up
as a blind date.

00:01:02.361 --> 00:01:07.534
And I was so shy
that I came into the house,

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:10.202
and I went downstairs
to be with the children,

00:01:10.202 --> 00:01:11.637
you know, play with them

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:14.106
until I had to sit down
at the table.

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:16.509
And, you know, I expected a guy

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.978
with a brown beard
and a brown hair --

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:21.747
wavy hair, like a mountain man.

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:26.686
And I saw a guy that had
all his hair cut off, no beard,

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:28.687
and I walked by him,

00:01:28.687 --> 00:01:31.090
I put my hand
through his hair and said,

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.492
"Who [bleep] up your hair?"

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.494
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:37.229
So, anyway, no,
it took me a few hours --

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:39.365
No, a few days.
A few days.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:40.900
-That's fantastic.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.470
And then, obviously,
you guys hit it off.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.804
25 years.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.940
And here's a little
granddaughter here

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.342
that's very excited
about your new album.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:51.811
Look at this. Come on.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:53.446
Look at that beautiful baby.

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.317
-Oh, my God. Well, her mom gave
her the album to look at Gammaw.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:58.617
You know?

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:02.088
The older one who is not even 3
calls me "Gammaw."

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:03.956
-Did you choose Gammaw,

00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:05.557
or she just ended up at Gammaw?

00:02:05.557 --> 00:02:08.728
Because my mother,
when I had our first child,

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:10.864
I said -- she goes,
"There's no way

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:12.432
you're gonna call me 'Grandma.'"

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:13.700
[ Laughter ]
-That's right.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.170
Most women say, "I don't want to
sound like a grandma

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.871
'cause it sounds like
I'm too old,"

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:19.872
but I love it.

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:21.341
I think it's a great gift.

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:24.376
I mean, I've lived, you know,
a long life.

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:26.212
I've done a lot of things,

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.081
but I've never been a gammaw.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.383
-[ Laughs ]
That's right.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.517
That's right.

00:02:31.517 --> 00:02:33.385
[ Applause ]

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:35.521
What a beautiful kid.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.423
"Release Me 2"

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.759
is unreleased songs throughout
your whole career.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:42.028
I freaked out.
I listened to it today.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.162
By the way,

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:44.597
I love the packaging on this.
-Oh, good.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:46.365
-You always do
something different,

00:02:46.365 --> 00:02:47.867
always something cool.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.135
So look at this.

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:52.204
This is the whole
kind of way it works.

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:54.874
Every different place that you
can pick up the album --

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:57.643
This is like Target.
You can get this one.

00:02:57.643 --> 00:03:00.513
This one, you get online
on your website.

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.514
Or that's this one.
Then this one --

00:03:02.514 --> 00:03:04.817
This one's for
independent record stores.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:05.985
-This one is --
-That's right.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.121
-I mean, Barnes and Noble.
This is genius.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:11.223
-Well, because I love things
that are off color,

00:03:11.223 --> 00:03:13.126
in more ways than one.

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:15.194
So these are not primary colors.

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:17.363
They're colors that I love.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:20.132
And, you know,
it took a long time.

00:03:20.132 --> 00:03:21.467
I mean, looking through

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.703
a hundred Pantone swatches,
you know?

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:24.570
-Yeah.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.805
-But I think it came out swell.

00:03:26.805 --> 00:03:28.508
-It came out better than swell.

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.809
In fact, I even got
a little swag with my album.

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:32.578
I don't know if you can
see here. Can we zoom in?

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:34.113
-Oh, let's see
what you're wearing.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:35.915
Oh! [ Laughs ]

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.282
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:37.282 --> 00:03:39.985
Oh, that's so cute.
Thank you.

00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:42.555
-[ Chuckles ]
And then not only --

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:43.956
Well, I want to talk
about the album,

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:45.592
but not only did you come
up with the vinyls,

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:48.394
but also on cassette,
which I love.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.796
No one does this.
Why cassette?

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:52.064
-You know why?

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:56.969
Because I still have
my old Sony cassette player

00:03:56.969 --> 00:04:00.573
that I've had for,
I don't know, 30, 40 years.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:04.676
And I love to record on it
because it's so simple --

00:04:04.676 --> 00:04:07.080
play, forward, backward,
that's it.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.283
The new stuff is so complicated.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:12.017
And I don't think
it's better sound.

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:15.287
The old sound,
the analog sound was so great,

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:16.855
and I still have it.

00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:18.558
-I love that you did that.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:23.229
I was listening today
because I wanted --

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.164
I knew I had you
on the show tonight.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.466
These are some songs from 1971.

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:30.402
You have a song from 1968.

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:34.606
I do love personally
"Rainbow Connection"

00:04:34.606 --> 00:04:35.808
with Kermit the Frog.

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:37.609
You do a duet
with Kermit the Frog.

00:04:37.609 --> 00:04:39.278
Two legends.
I mean, come on.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:40.713
But I love that.

00:04:40.713 --> 00:04:42.448
-That's why my A&amp;R said,

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:46.252
"You recorded with Judy Garland
and Frank Sinatra

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.487
and all these people,
and modern people, too --

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:50.289
Jamie Foxx, John Mayer."

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.825
He said, "But you've never sang
with a frog."

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.259
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.259 --> 00:04:56.762
-Yes. That's exactly right.
-Who is ageless.

00:04:56.762 --> 00:04:59.098
He said,
"Ageless and evergreen."

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:03.069
So it was really done
for my "Wet" album,

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:06.438
and yet, you know,
it was too --

00:05:06.438 --> 00:05:10.309
The "Wet" album was more
romantic songs, more sexy.

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:13.246
And it wasn't right for Kermit
until now.

00:05:13.246 --> 00:05:15.748
-Yeah, now it's right
for Kermit. I agree.

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.483
I also like --
-I sang this song

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.384
'cause it was my son's
favorite song

00:05:19.384 --> 00:05:20.953
when he saw "The Muppet Movie."

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:23.355
"The Muppet Movie." Yeah.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:23.355 --> 00:05:27.292
The one "If Only You Were Mine"
with Barry Gibb,

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:29.195
I listened to this
like four times today

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:31.330
because I like that there's
banter in there

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:32.965
that is just not pre-written.

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:34.900
It's just you and Barry
totally talking.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.135
You're like,
"This kind of sounds like

00:05:36.135 --> 00:05:37.570
'Singin' in the Rain'
a little bit."

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:38.938
And then he's going like --

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.807
It's fascinating.

00:05:41.807 --> 00:05:45.811
-Brutally authentic,
and that's what we recorded.

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:48.447
-And it says right here,
"Streisand,"

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.550
that's up
in the top corner here.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.586
Did you ever think that you just
have to go by Streisand?

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:56.856
Did you ever think
of a stage name ever?

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:59.058
Did anyone ever try
to convince you?

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:00.359
-Oh, yeah. Yeah.

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:04.262
People wanted me to be called
Barbra Sands.

00:06:04.262 --> 00:06:06.465
I thought, "What? No."

00:06:06.465 --> 00:06:09.802
Streisand is my name.
I don't want to change it.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.037
And I thought
for my first record,

00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:15.308
they wanted me to call it
"Sweet and Saucy Streisand."

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:18.211
I am neither
too sweet nor too saucy.

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:20.913
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:24.316
They saw the girl on TV,
my first show.

00:06:24.316 --> 00:06:27.987
I remember people were asking
where's my album.

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:29.889
So I said,
"Let's name it what it is --

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.557
'The Barbra Streisand Album.'"

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:32.825
-Yeah.
-That's what it is. You know?

00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:33.993
-And there you go.

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:36.495
I want to talk about

00:06:36.495 --> 00:06:38.163
projects you have
coming in the works.

00:06:38.163 --> 00:06:39.732
More with Barbra Streisand
when we come back.

